{"id":"2a7447c5-066c-43cc-9510-c87fb89e98b8","object":"chat.completion","created":1784884826,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    applications = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            name = p['name']\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            new_text = text.replace(old, new, 1)\\n            if new_text != text:\\n                text = new_text\\n                changed = True\\n                applications.append(name)\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': fixed_point, 'text': text}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-CR-01. The task description: \"Implement pass_scheduler_fixed_point through solve(case) for this bounded objective: Run an ordered transformation-pass schedule to a fixed point with registered restart, cache, and interestingness rules over a miniature program state.\"\n\nWe have a payload with fields: text, passes (list of objects with name, old, new), max_rounds, _case_nonce (probably not used). We need to simulate a simple C-Reduce-like pass scheduler. The passes are applied in order. Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep. Record only passes that change the artifact. Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed. Return the artifact (as text), application trace (list of pass names that changed the artifact), and fixed-point flag (boolean indicating whether we reached a fixed point, i.e., no changes in last sweep before max_rounds exhausted? Or if max_rounds reached, false? Typically fixed point means no pass changed the artifact in a sweep. If we stop because max_rounds reached, fixed_point=false. If we stop because no changes, fixed_point=true.)\n\nThe output schema requires: applications (array of strings, sequence of pass names that changed the artifact), fixed_point (boolean), text (string, the final artifact). So we need to simulate applying passes. For each sweep (round), we iterate passes in order. For each pass, we apply its rewrite: replace all occurrences of 'old' with 'new'? But description says \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" That suggests we only apply the first occurrence of the old pattern? Actually \"first eligible rewrite\" might mean we scan the text from left to right and replace the first occurrence of 'old' with 'new' (like sed s/old/new/ with one replacement). In C-Reduce, passes often replace the first matching pattern. Let's interpret: each pass in a sweep can replace at most one occurrence (the first) of its old substring with new. If multiple occurrences, only the first is replaced. If the pass changes the artifact, we record its name in applications list for that sweep? The output \"applications\" is a list of pass names that changed the artifact. But note: they want \"application trace\" - probably the sequence of passes that actually changed the artifact in order across all sweeps. But we need to confirm. The schema says \"applications\": array of strings. Likely it's the list of pass names that were applied (i.e., changed the text) in order. So if a pass is applied multiple times across different sweeps, we record it each time? Usually yes, the trace would include each application. However, the description: \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" That suggests we record each time a pass changes the artifact. So across sweeps, we accumulate a list of pass names each time they succeed. The final output should have that list.\n\nAlso, we need to consider: \"interestingness_preserved, restart_policy, cache_semantics\" are mentioned but not provided in payload. Maybe they are assumed to be always true or something? The payload only has passes and text and max_rounds. No interestingness, restart, cache fields. So we can ignore those. The task says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences.\" But since they are not in the input, we likely don't need to implement them. But maybe there is a default behavior: interestingness_preserved is always true? Restart policy: when a pass changes the artifact, do we restart from the beginning of the pass list? In C-Reduce, after a successful pass, the schedule restarts from the first pass. The description says \"registered restart\" - that likely means a restart policy where after any change, the next sweep starts from the first pass again. But we need to interpret. The text: \"Run an ordered transformation-pass schedule to a fixed point with registered restart, cache, and interestingness rules over a miniature program state.\" And \"registered restart\" could mean after a change, restart the sweep from the beginning? The standard C-Reduce behavior is to have fixed-point iteration with restart after each change (i.e., if a pass changes something, go back to the first pass). However, the description also says \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" That suggests we iterate passes in order within a sweep, and after a pass changes something, we continue to next pass in same sweep? Or restart? The phrase \"restart\" suggests we restart the sweep from the first pass after any change. But then \"per sweep\" would be confusing: a sweep is a full iteration over all passes. If we restart after each change, we never actually finish a sweep? That seems contradictory.\n\nLooking at typical C-Reduce: It has a list of passes. In each iteration (round), it goes through passes in order. If a pass modifies the program, it restarts from the first pass. This is to allow newly enabled reductions. This is called a \"restart\" policy. And the fixed-point is reached when a full sweep completes without any change. That matches \"Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed.\" So the description says \"Repeat full sweeps\" which implies we do sweeps, not restart within sweep. But then \"registered restart\" is present. Possibly the restart policy means that after a change, the next sweep restarts from the first pass (which is the normal behavior anyway). In typical fixed-point iteration, after a sweep with changes, you start the next sweep from the first pass. That's already implicit. So we may not need to implement intra-sweep restart.\n\nGiven the public fixtures, we can test our implementation. The fixtures have three identical cases: text \"aaaa bb cc\", passes: halve-a: replace \"aa\" with \"a\", drop-b: replace \"bb\" with \"b\", drop-c: replace \" cc\" with \"\" (note the leading space), max_rounds=8. We need to simulate.\n\nLet's manually trace. Start text: \"aaaa bb cc\"\nSweep 1:\n- pass halve-a: replace first occurrence of \"aa\" with \"a\". Text: \"aaa bb cc\" (first \"aa\" becomes \"a\", leaving \"aa\"? Actually \"aaaa\" has two overlapping \"aa\" at positions 0-1 and 2-3. First occurrence at 0-1 replaced with \"a\" yields \"a aa bb cc\"? Wait: original \"aaaa\" -> replace first \"aa\" with \"a\" gives \"a aa\" (since \"aa\" replaced by \"a\", the remaining is \"aa\" from positions 2-3). So \"a aa bb cc\" -> but careful: string replacement in Python: \"aaaa\".replace(\"aa\", \"a\", 1) gives \"aaa\". Actually Python's replace with count=1 replaces the first occurrence. For \"aaaa\", first \"aa\" at index 0-1, replace with \"a\" gives \"aaa\" (the remaining \"a\"+\"a\"? Actually after replacement, the rest of string is \"aa\", so result \"a\" + \"aa\" = \"aaa\". So \"aaaa\" becomes \"aaa\". So text becomes \"aaa bb cc\". So halve-a changes, record it.\n- pass drop-b: replace first \"bb\" with \"b\". Text: \"aaa bb cc\" -> first \"bb\" at \" bb\", replace with \"b\" gives \"aaa b cc\". Changed, record.\n- pass drop-c: replace first \" cc\" with \"\". Text: \"aaa b cc\" -> first \" cc\" at the end, replace with \"\" gives \"aaa b\". Changed, record.\nSweep 2: text \"aaa b\"\n- halve-a: replace first \"aa\" with \"a\". \"aaa\" -> first \"aa\" becomes \"a\" gives \"aa b\". Changed, record.\n- drop-b: replace first \"bb\"? There is no \"bb\". Text \"aa b\" has \"b\" but pattern is \"bb\". No match. No change.\n- drop-c: replace first \" cc\"? No match. No change.\nSweep 3: text \"aa b\"\n- halve-a: replace first \"aa\" with \"a\" -> \"a b\". Changed, record.\n- drop-b: no match.\n- drop-c: no match.\nSweep 4: text \"a b\"\n- halve-a: no \"aa\" -> no change.\n- drop-b: no \"bb\" -> no change.\n- drop-c: no \" cc\" -> no change.\nNow a full sweep with no changes, so fixed point reached. Applications recorded: halve-a (sweep1), drop-b (sweep1), drop-c (sweep1), halve-a (sweep2), halve-a (sweep3). So applications = [\"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\", \"halve-a\", \"halve-a\"]? But note: in sweep1, halve-a applied first, then drop-b, then drop-c. In sweep2, only halve-a. In sweep3, only halve-a. So the order should reflect the order applied: halve-a, drop-b, drop-c, halve-a, halve-a. That is contiguous? Actually after sweep1, we recorded all three. Then sweep2 halve-a, then sweep3 halve-a. So final list = [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\",\"halve-a\"]. But then fixed_point = True (since we reached no changes before max_rounds). text = \"a b\". This seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify with the given fixtures: all three have identical inputs. The \"output\" expected? Not given. But we can infer that our solve should return same result for all three. The nonce may be irrelevant. So that's a reasonable approach.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). case has keys: text (string), passes (list of dicts with name, old, new), max_rounds (int), _case_nonce (string, ignore). We need to iterate rounds up to max_rounds.