ere each pass is considered once. If a pass changes the artifact, we still continue to the next pass in the same sweep? That would be \"per sweep\" meaning per traversal. If we restart after change, you wouldn't have a concept of \"sweep\". But the next phrase: \"Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed.\" So they explicitly say \"full sweeps\". That implies we traverse all passes in order, and we only start a new sweep when the current sweep is completed. So intra-sweep restart is not used. The \"registered restart\" might refer to the behavior of restarting from the first pass when starting a new sweep? That is trivial. So I'll go with the \"full sweeps\" interpretation.\n\nGiven that the fixtures have three identical cases, we should produce the same output for all. Let's simulate with our \"full sweeps\" method: we got applications length 5, text \"a b\", fixed_point true. That seems consistent.\n\nBut we need to consider if there could be overlapping replacements causing issues. For example, halve-a: replace \"aa\" with \"a\". In sweep1, \"aaaa\" -> after replacing first \"aa\", we get \"aaa\". That's fine.\n\nNow, what about max_rounds? If we exceed max_rounds without reaching fixed point, we stop and set fixed_point=False. In this case, we reached fixed point at round 4, within 8 rounds.\n\nEdge cases: if passes list empty, no changes, text remains same, applications empty, fixed_point=True.\n\nWe must return exactly the JSON schema with three keys: applications (list of strings), fixed_point (bool), text (string). Use Python's json.dumps? The function should return a Python dictionary that is JSON-serializable (i.e., dict). The output of solve should be a dict conforming to schema.\n\nImplementation: def solve(case): text = case['text']; passes = case['passes']; max_rounds = case['max_rounds']; applications = []; fixed_point = False; for round in range(max_rounds): changed = False; for p in passes: name = p['name']; old = p['old']; new = p['new']; new_text = text.replace(old, new, 1); if new_text != text: text = new_text; changed = True; applications.append(name); if not changed: fixed_point = True; break; return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}.\n\nBut we must ensure we only replace first occurrence. Python str.replace(old, new, count=1) does that.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source string. Must escape newlines, quotes, backslashes. We'll construct the function code as a string.\n\nWe also need to consider that \"case\" may have additional fields? The schema says only use fields present. We'll assume text, passes, max_rounds are present. We'll access them directly.\n\nEdge case: passes might be in different order? The order is given.\n\nNow produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1195,"completion_tokens":3443,"total_tokens":4638,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3257},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":171},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}